{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"audio bus audio bus","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","fileName":"Audio Bus.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Audio Bus","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-20 23:34\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Audio Programming]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Audio Programming","Audio Snapshot","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"audio bus audio bus","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","fileName":"Audio Bus.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Audio Bus","heading":"Audio Bus","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An audio bus groups sound events into a mix category such as music, dialogue, effects, or ambience. Changing a bus affects every routed event, allowing independent volume control, muting, or processing without visiting each instance.\n\nBuses can form a hierarchy so a master bus governs the full mix while child buses retain category control. An [[Audio Snapshot]] can change several bus settings together for a temporary game state.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Audio Programming","Audio Snapshot","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"audio bus audio bus","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Audio Bus.md","fileName":"Audio Bus.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Audio Bus","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf]]","tags"